   "name": "Assets, liabilities and owner's equity"
  }
 ],
 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "• Assets are the resources owned by the business that have money value: land, buildings, equipment, furniture, vehicles, stock of goods, debtors (customers who owe money), bank and cash.\n• Liabilities are the debts of the business to outsiders: bank loans, creditors (suppliers not yet paid), unpaid bills.\n• Owner's equity (capital) is the owner's share in the business: what the owner put in, plus profits kept in the business, minus what the owner has taken out (drawings).\nDebtors are assets (they will pay the business); creditors are liabilities (the business will pay them). Do not mix them up!",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "d": "debtors",
   "c": "creditors"
  },
  "problem": "Explain the difference between {d} and {c}.",
  "steps": [
   "{d}: customers who owe the business money; they will pay it, so they are an asset.",
   "{c}: suppliers to whom the business owes money; the business must pay them, so they are a liability."
  ],
  "answer": "{d} = asset (they owe us); {c} = liability (we owe them).",
  "checks": []
 },
